## Examples (Ví dụ)

### Ví dụ: Order Processing Event-Driven Architecture

**Architecture:**

```
User Service → Order Created Event → [Exchange: orders]
                                              ↓
                    ┌─────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┐
                    ↓                         ↓                         ↓
           [Queue: email-notify]     [Queue: inventory-reserve]  [Queue: payment-process]
                    ↓                         ↓                         ↓
           Email Consumer            Inventory Consumer           Payment Consumer
```

**Implementation:**

```typescript
// Publisher: Order Service
class OrderEventPublisher {
  async publishOrderCreated(order: Order): Promise<void> {
    const event = {
      type: 'ORDER_CREATED',
      orderId: order.id,
      userId: order.userId,
      items: order.items,
      total: order.total,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    };
    
    await this.channel.publish(
      'orders.exchange',
      'order.created',
      Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(event)),
      {
        persistent: true,
        contentType: 'application/json',
        messageId: generateUUID(),
        headers: {
          'x-event-type': 'ORDER_CREATED',
          'x-schema-version': '1.0'
        }
      }
    );
  }
}

// Consumer: Email Notification
class EmailNotificationConsumer {
  async handle(event: OrderCreatedEvent): Promise<void> {
    const emailJob = await this.scheduler.add('send-order-email', {
      to: event.userId,
      template: 'order-confirmation',
      data: {
        orderId: event.orderId,
        items: event.items,
        total: event.total
      }
    });
    
    logger.info('Email job scheduled', { 
      jobId: emailJob.id, 
      orderId: event.orderId 
    });
  }
}

// Consumer: Inventory Reservation
class InventoryConsumer {
  async handle(event: OrderCreatedEvent): Promise<void> {
    for (const item of event.items) {
      await this.inventoryService.reserve({
        productId: item.productId,
        quantity: item.quantity,
        orderId: event.orderId
      });
    }
  }
}

// Dead Letter Handler
class DeadLetterConsumer {
  async handle(message: DeadLetteredMessage): Promise<void> {
    logger.error('Message sent to DLQ', {
      originalQueue: message.headers['x-original-queue'],
      reason: message.headers['x-death-reason'],
      message: JSON.parse(message.content.toString())
    });
    
    // Alert operations
    await this.alertService.notify({
      severity: 'high',
      title: 'Message processing failed',
      details: message.headers
    });
    
    // Store for manual retry
    await this.deadLetterStore.save(message);
  }
}
```

## Anti Pattern

- [ ] **No Acknowledgment**: Messages never acked
- [ ] **Synchronous Publishing**: Blocking on confirm
- [ ] **No Dead Letter**: Failed messages lost
- [ ] **Unbounded Queue**: Memory exhaustion
- [ ] **Single Consumer**: No parallelism
- [ ] **No Idempotency**: Duplicate processing
- [ ] **Tight Coupling**: Direct dependencies
- [ ] **No Backpressure**: Overwhelmed consumers

## Best Practice

- [ ] **Always ack/nack**: Never leave messages unacknowledged
- [ ] **Use dead letter queues**: Capture failed messages
- [ ] **Implement retries**: With exponential backoff
- [ ] **Idempotent consumers**: Handle duplicates safely
- [ ] **Batch processing**: Improve throughput
- [ ] **Monitor queue depth**: Alert on backlog
- [ ] **Connection pooling**: Reuse connections
- [ ] **Graceful shutdown**: Finish processing

